+# QA report: "You're subscribed" but no email arrives
+
+**Verdict:** Confirmed bug. The signup page shows a success message for addresses that the server rejects. Those people are never added to the subscriber list, so they never get an email.
+
+## Root cause
+
+`public/app.js` awaits `fetch('/api/subscribe')` but never looks at the response. Any HTTP response counts as success, including `422`. Only a network-level exception reaches the `catch` branch. The page then:
+
+- shows "You're subscribed. Check your inbox for the first issue."
+- clears the field (`form.reset()`), so the user can't see or correct what they typed.
+
+The server (`server.js` `validate()`) rejects with 422:
+
+- **local parts that contain `+`**, e.g. `jane+news@example.com`. This is a common, valid address style (Gmail/Fastmail sub-addressing). It is probably the main source of real-world reports.
+- **domains without a 2+ letter TLD**, e.g. `jane@localhost`. The browser's `type="email"` check accepts these, so the form submits.
+
+## Browser evidence (Chromium headless, Playwright, `node server.js` on a random port)
+
+| Email typed | API status / body | Status text shown to user | Field after submit |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| `jane@example.com` | 201 `{"ok":true}` | You're subscribed… | cleared |
+| `Jane@Example.COM` | 201 `{"ok":true}` | You're subscribed… | cleared |
+| `jane+news@example.com` | **422** `{"error":"Addresses with + are not supported."}` | **You're subscribed…** | cleared |
+| `jane@localhost` | **422** `{"error":"Enter a valid email address."}` | **You're subscribed…** | cleared |
+
+- Console: one `Failed to load resource: … 422 (Unprocessable Entity)` error for each rejected submit. Nothing else.
+- Failed network requests (`requestfailed`): none. The 422s are complete HTTP responses.
+
+## Regression test
+
+`e2e/subscribe.test.js` uses `node:test` with Playwright. It starts `node server.js` itself on `PORT=0` and shuts it down afterwards.
+
+```
+node --test e2e/subscribe.test.js
+```
+
+- `a valid address is accepted and the user is told so`: **passes**. It also asserts no console errors and no failed requests.
+- `a rejected address (jane+news@example.com) is not shown as subscribed`: **fails**. Message: "UI claimed success although the API returned 422".
+- `a rejected address (jane@localhost) is not shown as subscribed`: **fails**, same message.
+
+The rejected-address tests also expect the server's error text to appear in the status line and the typed address to stay in the field.
+
+**Does the test have teeth?** I copied the app to a scratch directory and patched `app.js` there to check `res.ok`, show `data.error` and skip `form.reset()` on failure. All 3 tests passed against that copy. The repo's app was not modified.
+
+## Suggested fix (not applied)
+
+1. In `app.js`, check `res.ok`. On failure, show the server's `error` message and don't reset the form.
+2. Product decision: reconsider rejecting `+` addresses. They are valid and widely used. Even with fix 1, those users are turned away instead of being subscribed.
+
+## Other observations (not verified as causes)
+
+- `subscribers` is an in-memory `Set` and the server never sends any email. Subscriptions are lost on restart. The "check your inbox" promise depends on whatever downstream process is meant to exist, and I couldn't see one in this repo.
+- The mutation check (`mutate.mjs`) was not run because it only mutates lines changed against `HEAD`, and no app code was changed.
